The Security Council,

Recalling its resolutions 2042 (2012), 2043 (2012), 2118 (2013), 2139 (2014), 2165 (2014), 2175 (2014), 2191 (2014), 2209 (2015), 2235 (2015), 2254 (2015), 2258 (2015), 2268 (2016), 2286 (2016), 2332 (2016), 2336 (2016), 2393 (2017), 2401 (2018), 2449 (2018), 2504 (2020), 2533 (2020), 2585 (2021), 2642 (2022), and 2672 (2023), and the Presidential Statements of 3 August 2011 (S/PRST/2011/16), 21 March 2012 (S/PRST/2012/6), 5 April 2012 (S/PRST/2012/10), 2 October 2013 (S/PRST/2013/15), 24 April 2015 (S/PRST/2015/10), 17 August 2015 (S/PRST/2015/15), and 8 October 2019 (S/PRST/2019/12),

Reaffirming its strong commitment to the sovereignty, independence, unity and territorial integrity of Syria and to the purposes and principles of the Charter of the United Nations,

Determining that the devastating humanitarian situation in Syria continues to constitute a threat to peace and security in the region, and deeply concerned by the further deterioration of the humanitarian situation, compounded by the severe impact of the earthquakes of 6 February 2023,

Underscoring that humanitarian activities are broader than solely the provision of basic necessities, and should include support to essential services through early recovery projects,

1. Demands the full and immediate implementation of all provisions of all relevant Security Council resolutions, including resolutions 2139 (2014), 2165 (2014), 2191 (2014), 2258 (2015), 2332 (2016), 2393 (2017), 2401 (2018), 2449 (2018), 2504 (2020), 2533 (2020), 2585 (2021), 2642 (2022), and 2672 (2023);

2. Decides to extend the decisions in paragraphs 2 and 3 of Security Council resolution 2165 (2014), for a period of nine months, that is, until 10 April 2024, only for the border crossing of Bab al-Hawa, and requests the Secretary-General to provide a special report on humanitarian needs in Syria no later than 10 March 2024;

3. Calls upon all Member States to respond with practical steps to address the urgent needs of the Syrian people in light of the profound socioeconomic and humanitarian impact of the conflict and recent earthquakes, and welcomes ongoing efforts and urges further initiatives to broaden humanitarian activities in Syria, including in water, sanitation, health, education, electricity where essential to restore access to basic services, and shelter early recovery projects;

4. Welcomes the ongoing efforts to expand cross-line humanitarian assistance across all parts of Syria and calls on all relevant parties to further promote, respect and fulfill humanitarian access to all persons in need across the country, in line with United Nations humanitarian principles;

5. Requests the Secretary-General to brief the Council monthly and to provide a report on a regular basis, at least every 60 days, on the implementation of resolutions 2139 (2014), 2165 (2014), 2191 (2014), 2258 (2015), 2332 (2016), 2393 (2017), 2401 (2018), 2449 (2018), 2504 (2020), 2533 (2020), 2585 (2021), 2642 (2022), 2672 (2023) and this resolution, and on humanitarian access throughout Syria;

6. Decides to remain actively seized of the matter.

